{#
  The coverage × depth grid — BRIEF.md §10: "Products, prices, durations, coverage and
  depth in one data file. The coverage × depth grid renders from it, including the
  not offered cells."

  Rendered on both Home and Services. The two hand-authored copies in the handover had
  already drifted by one cell (CHANGES.md #3), which is the argument for this macro in
  one line.
#}

{% macro grid(products) %}
{%- set path = products.products | product("top-up") %}
{#-
  `scroller--reflow` marks the ONE scroller that stops scrolling below 46rem, because §8's
  reflow applies to this table and to no other. There are fourteen scrollers on this site —
  eleven of them on the rubric page — and an unscoped `overflow-x: visible` took the scroll
  container away from all of them while only the two grid ones had been reflowed. Check 13
  caught it as 7px of page overflow on `/changelog/`, which is a table this session never
  touched and had no business moving.
-#}
<div class="scroller scroller--reflow" tabindex="0">
  <table class="grid">
    <caption>{{ products.grid.caption }}</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        {#-
          `.vh`, not `.skip`. This is a visually hidden heading for a screen reader; `.skip`
          is the skip-link class, which carries a `:focus` rule that reveals it at the top
          left of the page and a print rule that removes it. It hid this heading only by
          coincidence of both being off-screen.

          Harmless while chrome and content are styled by the same sheet, and not harmless
          after that: `.skip` is a chrome selector, so check 27 reports it reaching inside
          `<main>` on two pages, and a class shared between chrome and content is one the
          chrome derivation must either carry into every frozen page or drop from one.
        -#}
        <th scope="col"><span class="vh">{{ products.grid.coverageHeading }}</span></th>
        {%- for column in products.grid.columns %}
        <th scope="col">{{ column }}</th>
        {%- endfor %}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {%- for row in products.grid.rows %}
      {%- if row.type == "coverage" %}
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" class="rowhead">{{ row.head }}</th>
        {%- for key in row.cells %}
        {#-
          The depth label, repeated into every cell. `display: none` above 46rem, where the
          column header carries it and this would be a duplicate in the accessibility tree;
          shown below 46rem, where §8's reflow stacks the cells and `thead` goes.

          Markup rather than `content: attr(...)` deliberately. Generated content is not a
          DOM text node, so `getComputedStyle` cannot report its colour and check 7 is
          structurally unable to measure it — the exact hole that let a separator ship at
          1.37:1 (CHANGES.md row 49) and the reason §5.4 banned it in the footer. A label
          this reflow makes load-bearing must be measurable.

          It is not copy: the string is `products.grid.columns`, the same array the header
          row prints, so the two cannot disagree.
        -#}
        {%- set depth = products.grid.columns[loop.index0] %}
        {%- if key %}
        {%- set p = products.products | product(key) %}
        <td><span class="depth">{{ depth }}</span><span class="prod">{{ p.name }}</span><span class="scope"><span class="v">{{ p | price }}</span>&nbsp;<span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span> <span class="v">{{ p.duration }}</span></span></td>
        {%- else %}
        <td class="none"><span class="depth">{{ depth }}</span>{{ products.grid.notOffered }}</td>
        {%- endif %}
        {%- endfor %}
      </tr>
      {%- else %}
      <tr>
        <th scope="row" class="rowhead"><span class="path">&darr; {{ path.name }} &middot; {{ path | price }} &middot; {{ path.duration }}</span></th>
        <td class="path">{{ row.note }}</td>
        <td></td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      {%- endif %}
      {%- endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
{% endmacro %}
